          <p>
            A good strategy for regular users is two tubs: a dessert-style flavor you enjoy on its own, and a vanilla or unflavored powder for cooking and smoothies. That avoids flavor fatigue, which is the main reason people stop finishing tubs. Rotating flavors every month or so does the same job, and most of the brands on this list sell their popular flavors in smaller sizes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="make-it-taste-better" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Make Any Protein Powder Taste Better
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most bad protein shakes are a preparation problem rather than a product problem. The most common mistake buyers describe is too little liquid. Most labels suggest roughly 6-8 fluid ounces of liquid per scoop, but many people prefer 10-12 ounces, which dilutes both the sweetness and any off notes. If a powder tastes too sweet, add liquid. If it tastes watery, use less liquid or switch from water to milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Milk versus water is the biggest single upgrade. Dairy milk adds fat, natural sweetness and body, which turns a thin isolate into something closer to a milkshake, and it adds about 8 g of protein per cup. The trade-off is calories, roughly 80-150 per cup depending on fat content. Unsweetened almond milk adds body with few calories, while oat and soy milk are creamier. Cold matters too: almost every protein tastes better ice cold, and warm shakes taste noticeably more of the protein base.
          </p>

          <p>
            Order of mixing affects clumps. Pour liquid first, then powder, then shake hard for 20-30 seconds. A shaker bottle with a wire whisk ball or mesh insert gets most powders smooth. A blender is better still, especially for plant proteins and casein, and it lets you add ice, which thickens the shake and keeps it cold. Half a frozen banana, a spoon of peanut butter, a pinch of salt or a few drops of vanilla extract can rescue a disappointing tub.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
